✅ Confirmed as addressed by @melker22

**🩺 Stability & Availability** | **🟠 Major** | **⚡ Quick win**

**Limit the recursion depth of the expression parser.**

`parseUnary` calls itself once for each `!`. `parsePrimary` calls `parseOr` once for each `(`, which adds about five stack frames for each level. No depth limit exists. Expressions come from open boundaries:
- MCP `field_evaluate`, `hide_if`, and `show_if` arguments.
- Imported ODT `text:condition` attributes (`ooowToExpr`).
- DOCX `IF` instructions.
- `.aio.json` files.

An input such as 100 000 `(` characters or `!` characters overflows the stack. The process then crashes during layout, `document_get` (`refreshIfFields`), or `validate`. A crafted document therefore crashes the editor when it is opened and rendered. Add a depth counter and fail with `invalid expression` above a fixed limit.

<details>
<summary>🛡️ Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
     Val parseUnary()
     {
         skip();
         if (i_ < s_.size() && s_.at(i_) == QLatin1Char('!') && !startsWith(QStringLiteral("!="))) {
             ++i_;
+            if (++depth_ > kMaxDepth) {
+                fail();
+                return fromString({});
+            }
             const Val v = parseUnary();
+            --depth_;
             if (!error_.isEmpty())
                 return v;
             return fromBool(!isTruthyVal(v));
         }
         return parsePrimary();
     }
@@
         if (c == QLatin1Char('(')) {
             ++i_;
+            if (++depth_ > kMaxDepth) {
+                fail();
+                return fromString({});
+            }
             const Val v = parseOr();
+            --depth_;
```

Add these members next to `error_`:

```cpp
    int depth_ = 0;
    static constexpr int kMaxDepth = 256;
```
</details>











✅ Addressed in commits 215b5be to 67ab21c

**📐 Maintainability & Code Quality** | **🟡 Minor** | **⚡ Quick win**

**Forward-declare `Document` as `struct`, not `class`.**

`Document.h` defines `struct Document`. This header declares `class Document;`. GCC and Clang only warn (`-Wmismatched-tags`). MSVC encodes `class` and `struct` differently in mangled names and reports C4099. `FieldExpr.cpp` includes this header before `Document.h`. Other translation units include `Document.h` first. On MSVC, the two kinds of translation unit can then produce different mangled names for `evaluate`, `blockHiddenByExpr`, and the other functions that take `const Document &`, and linking fails. Use the class-key that the definition uses.

```diff
--- "a/src/core/FieldExpr.h"
+++ "b/src/core/FieldExpr.h"
@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@

 namespace aioffice {

-class Document;
+struct Document;
 struct Block;
 struct TextRun;

```

















✅ Addressed in commits 215b5be to 67ab21c

**🗄️ Data Integrity & Integration** | **🟠 Major** | **🏗️ Heavy lift**

**Matching by fingerprint alone can attach a conditional to a TOC entry or to an identical earlier block.**

`rememberConditionals` records `id`, `fp`, and `run_text`. On import, `applyFieldConditionals` scans from `boundCi + 1`, or from 0 for the first record. It takes the first block with the same fingerprint. Block type and occurrence are not checked. This causes two wrong matches:

- **TOC entries.** `saveDocx` writes each TOC entry as a `TOC1` paragraph with the text `t->plainText()`. On import, each entry becomes a `toc` block whose text equals the text of its heading, so the fingerprints are equal. TOC blocks come before the headings. A `hide_if` record on a listed heading therefore goes to the TOC entry. A non-native `if` field in a heading has the same problem: the entry contains the field result as a plain run, so `plainRun` also matches it. The heading loses the expression, and the TOC entry gets it.
- **Repeated text.** Assume an empty paragraph with no conditional comes before an empty paragraph with `hide_if`. The search starts at block 0, so the expression goes to the first empty paragraph.

In both cases the reopened document hides or evaluates the wrong content, and no error is reported. The TOC test in `qa/wave18-review.json` does not catch this because its `hide_if` paragraph is not a heading.

Store the block type and the occurrence number of this (type, fingerprint) pair in export order. On import, skip candidates with a different type. Pick the n-th candidate with the same type and fingerprint. TOC expansion only adds `toc` blocks, so the counts for other types stay the same.

<details>
<summary>🛡️ Proposed fix (sketch)</summary>

```diff
-void rememberConditionals(QJsonArray &out, const Block &b)
+void rememberConditionals(QJsonArray &out, const Block &b, QHash<QString, int> &seen)
 {
     const QString fp = blockTextFingerprint(b.plainText());
+    const int nth = seen[b.type + QLatin1Char('\x1f') + fp]++;
     if (!b.hideIf.isEmpty()) {
         QJsonObject o;
         o.insert(QStringLiteral("id"), b.id);
         o.insert(QStringLiteral("fp"), fp);
+        o.insert(QStringLiteral("type"), b.type);
+        o.insert(QStringLiteral("nth"), nth);
```

Add `type` and `nth` to the field records too. In `applyFieldConditionals`, count candidates with the same (type, fp) in import order. Accept only the candidate at index `nth`. Keep `plainRun` as the run-level check.
</details>






