✅ Addressed in commits 0f4bcae to 081be1d

**🎯 Functional Correctness** | **🟠 Major** | **🏗️ Heavy lift**

**Mixed-language paragraphs lose run formatting, drop caps, and ruby.**

`foreignRunPieces` returns every piece of the block when any run is foreign. Pieces in the document language are included with an empty `lang`. When `m.pieceLangs` is non-empty, `paintTextMarker` therefore draws the whole paragraph with `pdfDrawText(p, QPointF, substring)`. That call uses the block font `f` and pen `col`. The early `return` then skips the drop cap and `paintRuby`.

This happens for any paragraph that contains a run with a different `lang`, such as the `en-US` run in `wave19-pdf-ua.json`. It affects both the screen preview (`paintPage`/`renderPage`) and the PDF export:
- Bold, italic, size, colour, underline, link colour and highlight from `QTextLayout::setFormats` are not drawn.
- Justified lines lose their spacing, because the substring is drawn with natural advances from `cursorToX(a)`.
- In RTL or bidi lines, `cursorToX(a)` gives the logical start position. A left-to-right `drawText` from that position overflows or reorders the glyphs.
- The drop cap and the ruby annotations are not drawn.

Draw the shaped glyph runs of each piece instead of re-shaping a plain substring. `QTextLine::glyphRuns(from, length)` keeps the formats and positions from the layout. If one piece produces more than one glyph run (for example, one run per format), the BT/ET count no longer matches `pieceLangs`. The post-processor then falls back to one marked-content sequence, which is safe. For a 1:1 match, compute `pieceLangs` per glyph run in `pieceLangsFor`. Keep the drop cap and ruby on the split path as well.

<details>
<summary>Proposed direction</summary>

```diff
             for (const auto &pc : pieces) {
                 const int a = qMax(pc.d0, ls);
                 const int bend = qMin(pc.d1, le);
                 if (a >= bend)
                     continue;
-                p->setFont(f);
-                p->setPen(col);
-                pdfDrawText(p, QPointF(origin.x() + line.cursorToX(a), origin.y() + line.y() + line.ascent()),
-                            btl.display.mid(a, bend - a));
+                const auto runs = line.glyphRuns(a, bend - a,
+                    QTextLayout::RetrieveGlyphIndexes | QTextLayout::RetrieveGlyphPositions
+                        | QTextLayout::RetrieveStringIndexes | QTextLayout::RetrieveString);
+                pdfNoteGlyphRuns(runs);
+                for (const QGlyphRun &gr : runs)
+                    p->drawGlyphRun(origin, gr);
             }
         }
-        return;
+        paintRuby(p, btl, b, lb, col);
+        return;
     }
```
</details>

















✅ Confirmed as addressed by @melker22

**🎯 Functional Correctness** | **🟡 Minor** | **⚡ Quick win**

**`isBcp47` rejects valid and common language tags, so the wrong language is declared.**

The regex `^[A-Za-z]{2,3}(-[A-Za-z0-9]{2,8})*$` has two gaps:

- **Underscores:** it rejects `pt_BR` and `en_US`. Qt locale names and many importers use this form.
- **Extension and private-use subtags:** it rejects tags that contain a singleton, such as `en-x-custom` or `de-u-co-phonebk`.

`saveEpub` then replaces the document language with `pt-PT`. As a result, a Brazilian Portuguese or English document gets `xml:lang`, `lang`, and `dc:language` values of `pt-PT`. In `writeRuns`, `usableLang` also drops run languages in these forms.

Change underscores to hyphens first. Then accept a singleton when at least one 1–8 character subtag follows it.

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
--- "a/src/core/Epub.cpp"
+++ "b/src/core/Epub.cpp"
@@ -41,19 +41,21 @@
     return QStringLiteral("pt-PT");
 }

 bool isBcp47(const QString &tag)
 {
-    static const QRegularExpression re(QStringLiteral("^[A-Za-z]{2,3}(-[A-Za-z0-9]{2,8})*$"));
+    static const QRegularExpression re(QStringLiteral(
+        "^[A-Za-z]{2,3}(-[A-Za-z0-9]{2,8})*(-[A-WY-Za-wy-z0-9](-[A-Za-z0-9]{2,8})+)*(-[Xx](-[A-Za-z0-9]{1,8})+)?$"));
     return re.match(tag).hasMatch();
 }

 QString usableLang(const QString &raw, const QString &fallback)
 {
-    const QString t = raw.trimmed();
+    QString t = raw.trimmed();
+    t.replace(QLatin1Char('_'), QLatin1Char('-'));
     if (isBcp47(t))
         return t;
     return fallback;
 }

 bool langIsRtl(const QString &lang)
 {
```

Also change `saveEpub` to use `usableLang(primaryRaw, QStringLiteral("pt-PT"))` in place of `isBcp47(primaryRaw)`.
</details>

Based on learnings: "normalize underscores to hyphens before validation" and "a singleton subtag ... introduces an extension/private-use section, after which subsequent 1-8 character alphanumeric subtags are valid."










